WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.730 line:-6
So our ultimate goal is for any Tier 1 support question to be able to answer without human interaction.

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:11.997 line:-6
That's the overall goal.

00:00:11.997 --> 00:00:23.805 line:-6
And then below that, there's an additional goal of time to resolution that we looked to influence and time to first response.

00:00:23.805 --> 00:00:32.117 line:-6
So you already know this, but Mach 1 can be an immediate response to a customer request.

00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:40.336 line:-6
But we have a set on a slight delay just to give it a little bit more of a human touch.

00:00:40.336 --> 00:00:44.775 line:-6
And so we have a set to, I think, four minutes.

00:00:44.775 --> 00:00:50.160 line:-6
Not a realistic human touch, but it's still four minutes.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:01:00.362 line:-6
And in the first few months after implementing Mach 1, our time to first contact dropped from multiple days.

00:01:00.362 --> 00:01:06.785 line:-6
Our team was that far behind on their support queue to four minutes.

00:01:06.785 --> 00:01:08.391 line:-6
It was immediate.

00:01:08.391 --> 00:01:11.225 line:-6
There wasn't a lag time in it.

00:01:11.225 --> 00:01:22.560 line:-6
So many of the inbound tickets were able to be handled by Mach 1 just from our launch that the ratio just shrank so far.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:24.261 line:-6
It was astounding.

00:01:24.261 --> 00:01:27.000 line:-6
It was absolutely astounding.

